## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce data residency and leakage controls at runtime with low impact, using Vault for policy and identity, and blue/green rollouts for safe deployments.

## Key Concepts
- Data-loss prevention in containerized traffic
- Vault integration and SPIFFE SVID rotation
- Tenant-scoped JSON schemas
- Dynamic policy updates and hot-reload
- Canary/Blue-green deployment patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal pseudo sidecar validator
const Validator = require('jsonschema').Validator;
const v = new Validator();
const tenant = process.env.TENANT_HEADER; // from request header
const schema = fetchVaultSchema(tenant); // resolves /secret/tenants/{tenant}/schema
v.addSchema(schema, '/tenant/schema');
const payload = getOutboundBody();
const result = v.validate(payload, { // simplified schema
  type: 'object', properties: { tenant: {type: 'string'}, data: {type: 'object'} }, required: ['tenant','data']
}, '/tenant/schema');
if (!result.valid) blockOrRedact(payload, result.errors);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test false positives/negatives per tenant?
- How would you handle schema evolution without breaking existing tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge routing with simple feature flag logic using standard nginx capabilities, keeping it beginner-friendly while still requiring practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- nginx upstreams and map for header-driven routing
- TLS termination in docker-compose
- cross-DC networking and service naming

## Code Example
```nginx
http {
  upstream api-old { server api-old:8080; }
  upstream api-new { server api-new:8080; }
  map $http_x_feature_flag_alpha $target { default api-old; 1 api-new; }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /certs/server.crt; ssl_certificate_key /certs/server.key;
    location / { proxy_pass http://$target; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test header-driven routing in CI?
- How would you add health checks and a safe rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to add a real, cross-DC capability using Docker Configs, a TLS edge (Traefik), and a simple stateful component (Redis). It also touches observability (metrics) and inter-DC consistency via pub/sub.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant rate limiting with Redis counters
- Docker Configs for per-tenant quotas
- Cross-DC synchronization via Redis Pub/Sub
- TLS termination with Traefik edge proxy
- Metrics exposure for Prometheus

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-rate-limit middleware sketch
const quota = getQuotaFromConfig(req.headers['X-Tenant']);
const key = `rl:${tenant}:${windowStart}`;
let count = redis.incr(key);
if (count === 1) redis.expire(key, windowSeconds);
if (count > quota) {
  res.status(429).set('Retry-After', String(windowSeconds)).send('Too Many Requests');
} else {
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test burst traffic and quota drift across DCs?
- What strategies ensure accurate time windows across replicas?
- How would you monitor per-tenant usage and alert on anomalies?
